s-6pphz"><div class=3D"ureact-markdown css-tc5hjw"><p class=3D"chakra-text =
css-o3oz8b">For this project, you can use any algorithm of your choosing to=
 solve the task.  You are strongly encouraged to do your own research, to d=
evise your own approach towards solving this problem.</p>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">In case you get stuck, here are the det=
ails of one approach that worked well for us.</p>
<h2 class=3D"chakra-heading css-fz7yxd">An Amended DDPG Agent</h2>
<hr aria-orientation=3D"horizontal" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"chakra-di=
vider css-bdm52a">
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">In this part of the Nanodegree program,=
 you learned about a lot of potential ways to solve this project.  We inste=
ad decided to solve the project by making some amendments to the Deep Deter=
ministic Policy Gradients (DDPG) algorithm.</p>
<h3 class=3D"chakra-heading css-k57syw">Attempt 1</h3>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">The first thing that we did was amend t=
he DDPG code to work for multiple agents, to solve version 2 of the environ=
ment.  The DDPG code in the DRLND GitHub repository utilizes only a single =
agent, and with each step:</p>
<ul role=3D"list" class=3D"css-19qh3zo"><li class=3D"css-1q0heax">the agent=
 adds its experience to the replay buffer, and</li><li class=3D"css-1q0heax=
">the (local) actor and critic networks are updated, using a sample from th=
e replay buffer.</li></ul>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">So, in order to make the code work with=
 20 agents, we modified the code so that after each step:</p>
<ul role=3D"list" class=3D"css-19qh3zo"><li class=3D"css-1q0heax">each agen=
t adds its experience to a replay buffer that is shared by all agents, and<=
/li><li class=3D"css-1q0heax">the (local) actor and critic networks are upd=
ated 20 times in a row (one for each agent), using 20 different samples fro=
m the replay buffer.</li></ul>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">In hindsight, this wasn't a great plan,=
 but it was a start!  That said, the scores are shown below.</p></div></div=
></div></div><div class=3D"css-8dn4zy"><div class=3D"css-0"><div class=3D"c=
ss-ivc47x"><div class=3D"css-1baulvz"><div class=3D"css-79elbk"><img width=
=3D"400" alt=3D"Line plot showing the score progression over episodes, with=
 a significant peak around episode 100 followed by a sharp decline and low =
scores thereafter." class=3D"chakra-image css-0" src=3D"https://video.udaci=
ty-data.com/topher/2018/July/5b4cbfa1_screen-shot-2018-05-02-at-4.56.45-pm/=
screen-shot-2018-05-02-at-4.56.45-pm.png"></div></div></div></div></div><di=
v class=3D"css-8dn4zy"><div class=3D"css-0"><div class=3D"css-6pphz"><div c=
lass=3D"ureact-markdown css-tc5hjw"><p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">You=
'll notice that we made some rapid improvement pretty early in training, be=
cause of the extremely large number of updates.  Unfortunately, also due to=
 the large number of updates, the agent is incredibly unstable.  Around epi=
sode 100, performance crashed and did not recover.</p>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">So, we focused on determining ways to s=
tabilize this first attempt.</p>
<h3 class=3D"chakra-heading css-k57syw">Attempt 2</h3>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">For this second attempt, we reduced the=
 number of agents from 20 to 1 (by switching to version 1 of the environmen=
t).  We wanted to know how much stability we could expect from a single age=
nt.  The idea was that the code would likely train more reliably, if we did=
n't make so many updates.  And it did train much better.</p></div></div></d=
iv></div><div class=3D"css-8dn4zy"><div class=3D"css-0"><div class=3D"css-i=
vc47x"><div class=3D"css-1baulvz"><div class=3D"css-79elbk"><img width=3D"4=
00" alt=3D"Line plot showing an increasing trend of scores over episodes wi=
th a sudden drop after episode 150." class=3D"chakra-image css-0" src=3D"ht=
tps://video.udacity-data.com/topher/2018/July/5b4cc314_screen-shot-2018-05-=
03-at-9.10.50-am/screen-shot-2018-05-03-at-9.10.50-am.png"></div></div></di=
v></div></div><div class=3D"css-8dn4zy"><div class=3D"css-0"><div class=3D"=
css-6pphz"><div class=3D"ureact-markdown css-tc5hjw"><p class=3D"chakra-tex=
t css-o3oz8b">At one point, we even hit the target score of 30.  However, t=
his score wasn't maintained for very long, and we saw strong indications th=
at the algorithm was going to crash again.  This showed us that we needed t=
o spend more time with figuring out how to stabilize the algorithm, if we w=
anted to have a chance of training all 20 agents simultaneously.</p>
<h3 class=3D"chakra-heading css-k57syw">Attempt 3</h3>
<p class=3D"chakra-text css-o3oz8b">This time, we switched back to version =
2 of the environment, and began with the code from <strong>Attempt 1</stron=
g> as a starting point.  Then, the only change we made was to use gradient =
clipping when training the critic network.  The corresponding snippet of co=
de was as follows:</p>
<pre class=3D"css-0"><div data-defines-codeblock=3D"true" tabindex=3D"0" cl=
ass=3D"css-ift61f"><div class=3D"css-12xh36r"><div class=3D"css-8mhztr"></d=
iv></div><div style=3D"color: rgb(15, 43, 61); text-shadow: white 0px 1px; =
font-family: &quot;Fira Code&quot;, Consolas, Monaco, &quot;Andale Mono&quo=
t;, &quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;, monospace; font-size: 1em; text-align: left; w=
hite-space: pre; word-spacing: normal; word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: n=
